rust-proxmox-schema (5.5.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * api types: anchor the end of the ED25519 key regex. Previously, any value
    starting with a valid base64 encoded key passed validation.

  * oneOf: select the active variant through the type property, so variants
    can use different schemas for the same property name, where previously the
    first variant's schema was used:
    - lookups, property iteration and the verification of property strings,
      parameters and JSON values follow the selected variant, also when the
      type comes after the properties of its variant.
    - unknown types are rejected.
    - add the lookup_schema, lookup_all and all_properties methods. The old
      lookup and properties methods are soft-deprecated, only warning when
      building with `--cfg proxmox_schema="strict"`.
    - documentation shows the condition under which a property applies.
    - serializing a property string requires the type property to come before
      the properties of its variant, as with serde's derived implementations.
    - a type property declared as optional is rejected at compile time.

  * property strings: verification tracks aliases like their canonical
    property, so an alias satisfies a required property and conflicts with the
    canonical name.

  * property strings: deserializing into a map, like a JSON value, rejects
    duplicate keys instead of keeping the last value, and validation errors no
    longer start with "schema validation failed: ".

  * drop the unused de::MapAccess, de::SeqAccess and de::verify::Verifier from
    the public API and give SchemaDeserializer a third lifetime parameter.
    de::verify replaces de::verify::verify, which is soft-deprecated.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:39:06 +0200

rust-proxmox-schema (5.4.2-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * api types: anchor both alternatives of the block device name regexes.
    Previously, any value starting with an sd, hd, vd or xvd disk name, or
    ending with an NVMe namespace name, passed validation, and the disk-only
    variant also accepted partitions.

